deficiency\n\nMyelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML (MDS-related or therapy-related) vs Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) with Richter's Transformation:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, bleeding, easy bruising, frequent infections\n\nMyelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML (MDS-related or therapy-related) vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic/accelerated Phase (Ph+, BCR-ABL1):\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nMyelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML (MDS-related or therapy-related) vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF) with leukemoid reaction:\n  Favours first:  abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, acute myeloid leukemia, anemia of inadequate production\n  Favours second: abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, anorexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nDe Novo Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) vs Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) with Richter's Transformation:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, cellular immunodeficiency\n  Favours second: abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, bleeding, easy bruising, pallor\n\nDe Novo Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic/accelerated Phase (Ph+, BCR-ABL1):\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, cellular immunodeficiency, chronic lymphatic leukemia\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nDe Novo Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF) with leukemoid reaction:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) with Richter's Transformation vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic/accelerated Phase (Ph+, BCR-ABL1):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, bleeding, easy bruising, frequent infections\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nChronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) with Richter's Transformation vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF) with leukemoid reaction:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, bleeding, bone pain, easy bruising\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic/accelerated Phase (Ph+, BCR-ABL1) vs Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF) with leukemoid reaction:\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, bleeding easily, blurred vision\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal megakaryocyte morphology, abnormal thrombosis, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, anorexia\n\nNOT typically seen in Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF) with leukemoid reaction: autoimmune antibody positivity, hemophagocytosis\n\nRelated to Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML (MDS-related or therapy-related): hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, myelodysplastic syndrome, myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts-1\n\nRelated to De Novo Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL): acute lymphoblastic leukemia (disease), acute lymphoblastic/lymphocytic leukemia, childhood leukemia\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic/accelerated Phase (Ph+, BCR-ABL1): blast phase chronic myelogenous leukemia, bcr-abl1 positive, familial chronic myelocytic leukemia-like syndrome, leukemia, myeloid, accelerated-phase\n\nRelated to Primary Myelofibrosis (PMF) with leukemoid reaction: acquired aplastic anemia, cellular phase chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis, familial myelofibrosis\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML (MDS-related or therapy-related)\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) in Chronic/accelerated Phase (Ph+, BCR-ABL1)"}
