ated WBC could be due to infection/stress, but LPAP is not typically elevated in benign states", "Stress-induced symptoms: anxiety history could explain fatigue, but does not account for WBC/LPAP abnormalities or hypercalcemia", "Primary hyperparathyroidism: hypercalcemia might suggest this, but lacks renal dysfunction and other parathyroid features", "Infectious aetiology: recent cold and constitutional symptoms are plausible, but leukocyte differential is normal and LPAP is elevated"], "alarm_features": ["Weight loss", "Hypercalcemia", "Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.13513513513513514, "posterior": 0.09015188633023027, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Subacute symptoms and elevated WBC without infection are nonspecific but align with myeloid processes. However, absence of blast count data prevents decisive s…"], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP is inconsistent with typical myeloid neoplasms but could occur in rare cases. The normal differential further weakens this possibility."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.2702702702702703, "posterior": 0.1558059774620284, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Hypercalcemia is more commonly associated with lymphoid malignancies, but the lack of lymphocytosis or blast count data prevents strong support for B2."], "evidence_against": ["Normal differential weakens lymphoid blast involvement, though constitutional symptoms remain nonspecific for B2."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.06614404703576676, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP and lack of splenomegaly moderately argue against CML blast crisis, though chronic-phase CML remains a consideration."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3", "B3.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.32432432432432434, "posterior": 0.15874571288584027, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Hypercalcemia is more commonly associated with lymphoid malignancies, but the lack of lymphocytosis or blast count data prevents strong support for B2."], "evidence_against": ["Normal peripheral blood differential is the strongest finding against lymphoproliferative disorders, as blasts or abnormal lymphocytes would be expected."], "unresol